# ── MEDIARR behind Caddy on a DuckDNS domain ───────────────────────────────
# 1) Point your DuckDNS subdomain at this machine's public IP (DuckDNS updater).
# 2) Run MEDIARR bound to localhost so only Caddy can reach it:
#       HOST=127.0.0.1 PORT=7575 node server.js
# 3) Put this file at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (replace MYNAME + the token), then:
#       sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Caddy gets a free HTTPS certificate automatically and forwards the real
# client protocol via X-Forwarded-Proto, which MEDIARR uses to set a Secure
# cookie. Nothing else needs configuring.
#
# IMPORTANT: proxy the WHOLE site to MEDIARR (one reverse_proxy, no file_server
# and no path matchers). MEDIARR serves its own HTML *and* its /api endpoints,
# so if Caddy serves files itself or only proxies some paths, /api/login returns
# an empty/HTML body and the login screen shows a JSON parse error.

# ── Option A: ports 80 + 443 are reachable from the internet (simplest) ──────
MYNAME.duckdns.org {
    encode zstd gzip
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7575
}

# ── Option B: your ISP blocks port 80, so use the DNS challenge instead ───────
# Requires a Caddy build that includes the DuckDNS DNS module, e.g.:
#       xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/duckdns
# (or download a Caddy build with "caddy-dns/duckdns" from caddyserver.com/download)
#
# MYNAME.duckdns.org {
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7575
#     tls {
#         dns duckdns YOUR_DUCKDNS_TOKEN
#     }
# }

# Notes
# • Replace MYNAME with your DuckDNS subdomain and YOUR_DUCKDNS_TOKEN with the
#   token from your DuckDNS account page.
# • If MEDIARR runs in Docker, change 127.0.0.1:7575 to the container's
#   host:port (e.g. mediarr:7575) and make sure Caddy is on the same network.
# • The app stays plain HTTP internally; Caddy handles all TLS.